<p>Russia said Tuesday, May 12, that it successfully test-launched its nuclear-capable Sarmat intercontinental ballistic missile, and President Vladimir Putin said the system would enter combat service by the end of 2026. Russian state television showed Strategic Missile Forces commander Sergei Karakayev reporting the test to Putin, and the Kremlin described the launch as part of Moscow’s effort to modernize its strategic nuclear forces. Putin called Sarmat “the most powerful missile system in the world” and said it had a range of more than 35,000 km and could fly a suborbital trajectory. The missile, known in the West as “Satan II,” was built to replace the Soviet-era Voyevoda and is designed to carry nuclear warheads to targets thousands of miles away in the United States or Europe. The test followed the February 2026 expiration of New START, the last U.S.-Russia treaty limiting the two countries’ strategic nuclear arsenals. Western security analysts cited by Reuters said Putin has exaggerated claims about some new Russian nuclear weapons, and several sources noted that Sarmat has had earlier setbacks, including a reported failed test in September 2024 that left a crater at a launch silo.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/eNWugcIZx5KP1Br2GBDVC5qRz3FPkhroTgPPjiCJSt3ftmt4VrciPjxL4pTkSMk18Q908WcvMxZAu0-zGzm36qwu-viTAgFdTdNX0C51qTAkOaaCPBfJMqZYmhOcHWryiq81r6mQMxg0qRLca9uZbRjOxh_x4PRFYHQG6GLXyiWwDkN0IHH1_df7KiZG84rFYexsIvjQmsVmjK7UnyFDb7C1lVVmWFh5x3WdS9aAXy_e83vJZXPX_ZZfJEnYD3UcqRmbQb6n_LDn-GEdXg' alt='Russian Strategic Missile Forces chief Col. Gen. Sergei Karakayev reports to President Vladimir Putin on the Sarmat test launch at the Kremlin in Moscow on May 12, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Launch path: The War Zone reported that the missile launched from Plesetsk Cosmodrome at 11:15 a.m. Moscow time, and Russian officials said it hit the Kura range on the Kamchatka Peninsula about half an hour later.</li><li>First regiment: Russian officials identified the Uzhur formation in Krasnoyarsk Krai as the first unit scheduled to receive Sarmat-armed launchers after the latest test.</li><li>Verification gap: BBC Russian said independent Western sources had not commented on the launch and that the missile’s flight trajectory was not yet known.</li></ul><blockquote>It has the ability to penetrate all existing and future anti-missile defence systems - President Vladimir Putin</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Sergei Karakayev, commander of the Strategic Missile Forces: Karakayev told Putin that deploying Sarmat-equipped launchers would significantly strengthen Russia’s ground-based strategic nuclear forces for deterrence and target-destruction missions. (<a href='https://www.brecorder.com/news/40420839/putin-says-russia-will-deploy-new-sarmat-nuclear-missile-this-year'>Business Recorder</a>)</li><li>Western officials: CNA reported that Western countries see Putin’s repeated emphasis on Russia’s nuclear arsenal since the 2022 invasion as an effort to deter deeper involvement in support of Ukraine. (<a href='https://www.cna.com.tw/news/aopl/202605130004.aspx'>CNA</a>)</li><li>Independent Russian media: Important Stories wrote that Putin has promised for several years to put Sarmat missiles on combat duty, but the weapons have not been deployed. (<a href='https://istories.media/news/2026/05/12/putin-snova-poobeshchal-postavit-sarmati-na-boevoe-dezhurstvo/'>Important Stories</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/russia-tests-nuclear-capable-satan-ii-missile-with-35-000-km-range-president-vladimir-putin-calls-most-powerful-missile-in-world-11487539#publisher=newsstand'>Russia Tests Nuclear-Capable &#x27;Satan II&#x27; Missile With 35,000 km Range</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/5/13/putin-hails-russias-test-launch-of-most-powerful-missile-in-the-world'>Putin hails Russia’s test launch of ‘most powerful missile in the world’</a> - aljazeera.